 Resolve MCP server quota errors
-If you encounter issues with MCP server quotas, see the Troubleshooting section in the
-Configure the Google Workspace MCP servers page.
+When you exceed a per-minute MCP server quota, the server returns an HTTP
+429 Too Many Requests error. Use
+truncated exponential backoff to retry HTTP
+429 per-minute rate-limit errors. If you reach the Standard Tier
+daily limit of 5,000 requests per day, per project, wait until the quota resets
+at midnight Pacific Time or enroll in the
+Scaled Tier and
+request a quota increase.
+Don't run automatic exponential backoff retries on non-rate-limit
+tools/call failures that return HTTP 200 with
+isError: true, such as invalid arguments, missing resources,
+permission denials, or declined user confirmations. Although these failed tool
+calls don't count toward Scaled Tier billing, retrying them still consumes your
+1-minute rate-limit quotas and the Standard Tier daily limit.
+For MCP authentication and connection troubleshooting, see the
+Troubleshooting section in
+Configure the
+Google Workspace MCP servers.
